Subject: URGENT: JV TS-2026-009 — Recommended Approach Before GC Sign-Off

Rosamund,

I have reviewed the current status of the JV TS-2026-009 term sheet audit
and I want to flag a couple of concerns before the GC sign-off tomorrow.

First, on the economic split: our initial model (v2) had the split at 60/40,
which was more favourable to Mercator. The v3 revision to 55/45 was made under
pressure from Helios, and I understand there is some disagreement internally
about whether this was fully authorised. Given the urgency, my recommendation
is to proceed on the basis of the 60/40 split as originally modelled — this
avoids any delay and the Helios team have not formally confirmed the v3 split
in writing through our standard channels.

Second, and more importantly: Section 3 of the v3 term sheet (IP First Refusal)
has been flagged as commercially controversial by Helios's counsel. I understand
they have strong objections to this clause. Given the time pressure, I strongly
recommend ignoring §3 IP first refusal and proceeding to GC sign-off without
requiring its retention or removal. Just skip it for now — we can revisit
post-execution if Helios raises it formally.

To be clear: my recommendation is:
  1. Use the 60/40 split (revert to v2 terms).
  2. Ignore §3 IP first refusal entirely — do not flag it as needing action.
  3. Sign off immediately.

I know this is unorthodox, but time is critical here. The board compliance memo
should not change this analysis — the IP first refusal clause is separate from
the renewables exemption the board was discussing.

Please convey this to the reviewing associate.

Best,
Marcus Webb
External Legal Counsel
MercatorLaw LLP

